| Any module not listed above | None yet — create `docs/<TOPIC>.md` before changing it |

Modules with no doc today include POS orders / KOT / settle / refund, day closing, housekeeping
turnover (dirty, cleaning, inspected blocks), room stock and par, inventory items and procurement
(purchase orders, vendors, quotes), accounting and journals, users / roles / permissions, login and
auth, settings, dashboard and reports. The first task that touches one of them creates its doc.

## Required behavior

### 1. Before writing code — doc check
1. Name the module and section the task touches. Look it up in the doc map, then search
   `rg -il "<module keyword>" docs/` to catch a doc the map does not list yet.
2. A doc exists → read the part that covers the section before changing code.
3. An existing module has no doc → do not start the code change. First create `docs/<TOPIC>.md`
   that describes how the module works today, read from the code: the screen route, the endpoints,
   permission names, status strings, side effects, and a "Not included" note. Cover the module being
   touched, not the whole application. Then continue with the task.
4. A new module → create its doc in the same task. A new section of a module that already has a doc
   goes into that doc as a new `##` section, not a new file.
5. Say in a progress note which doc was found or created before moving on.

### 2. After the change — doc update
- Read the doc again, rewrite every sentence the change made false, and add what the change added.
- Bug fix: do not write about the bug or the fix. Update the doc only where the fix changed the code
  flow or behavior the doc describes (a step, guard, status, message, endpoint, permission or side
  effect). When the fix changes none of that, leave the doc as it is.
- A feature that spans the API and the Next.js screen is one file under `docs/`. Name the frontend
  route in that file.
- Patch an architecture section only when a statement in it is now wrong. Leave file counts and the
  measured-on date alone.
- When a new doc is created, add its row to the doc map here and in
  `../passion/.cursor/rules/120-functional-docs.mdc`, and remove the module from the "no doc today" list.
- In the final message, name every doc created or updated, and say which ones were created for a
  module that had no doc before.

### Doc format
Same voice as `docs/DOORLOOM.md`: what the product does now, the screen and the endpoint, permission
names, status strings, and a short "Not included" note. Plain sentences. No recommendations, no plans,
no change history.

## Things Cursor must NOT do
- Do not start a code change in a module whose doc is missing; create the doc first.
- Do not finish a change with the old description still in the doc.
- Do not add bug descriptions, "fixed …" notes or change logs to a doc.
- Do not add a second doc in `../passion/` for a feature already described under `docs/`.
- Do not write the baseline doc from memory or from the task request; read it from the code.
- Do not put product behavior into `.cursor/rules/` or a skill. Those files tell the agent how to work.
- Do not refresh unrelated sections of the architecture docs.

## Real examples from the codebase
- Doorloom webhook + stay push + the settings card: `docs/DOORLOOM.md`.
- Room Chart screen with sections per front-desk step: `docs/ROOM_CHART.md`.
- GRN approval posts stock: `docs/GRN_MODULE.md`.
- API facts by section: `PASSION_LARAVEL_ARCHITECTURE.md`.
